Z||000002||Kwez||08-11-2001||03:30 PM||||a bit yes, but not as much as I would expect, maybe since the front brakes are doing most of the braking I can't feel it as much ?<P>I just did some braking tests on a nice flat road.<P>At 1st, nothing, after a couple of 'stops', the right side was doin' it (ahah ! the new rotor that was swapped to the right ?) ...<BR>but after more braking, both sides now do it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> (and stronger on the original side: left)<P>When both sides do it I can feel the whole car 'rocking' a bit more.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Primaris||08-11-2001||03:58 PM||||Well I don't understand how it can be comming and going.  But, the symptoms you describe are of a warped rotor. The only way I could see the problem comming and going if for some reason you rear brakes are not operating each time you stop.  You have an '88 right?  If so maybe swap the rear rotors to the front and see if it occurs each time you stop.  Oh, use your e-brake a few times.  This will adjust the rear pads outward. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Kwez||08-11-2001||05:09 PM||||hmmm, I guess I could try that. (yes it's an 88)<BR>(but my 'tools guy' is away for the week-end & is going back to work next week)<P>the rotors are brand new by the way !<P>as far as I know they did not overheat.<BR>I have one in the front that got hot a few times before I got the new front calipers (two short drives), the rotor was blueish, but not warped. <P>I did not get new calipers for the rear & the shop didn't mention anything wrong after installing the new rear rotors & pads.<P>"it comes & goes": it seems to start freakin' up as the brakes warm up, after braking a couple of times.<P>Since the car needed new calipers in the front, could it need calipers in the rear too ?<BR>Maybe the sliders are partially seized (sp?) & get worse as they heat up ?<BR>I remember that on one of the wheels (which one ???), one of the sliders was jammed but the other was (almost) ok.<P>also <P>It does seem to be worst on one side, even after swapping the rotors left - right ...<BR>and the front (with the new calipers) are OK ...<P>hehe, maybe if i keep posting more & more details it will begin to make sense    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Kwez (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000005||Raydar||08-11-2001||06:32 PM||||Definitely sounds like warped rotors.<BR>One thing to make sure of is that all your lugs are tightened evenly, and to the correct value. If you are missing lugs/studs, it causes an uneven pressure on the wheel/rotor/hub.<BR>Not sure exactly how this works, but I've heard this for years.<BR>You might try pulling the wheels and rotors off, and make sure there is no rust on any of the mating surfaces. Could cause different heat transfer characteristics around the rotor. <BR>Purely a shot in the dark.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Primaris||08-11-2001||06:42 PM||||Didn't you say you got one rotor pretty hot? Rotors often warp when you get them hot and park.  The area behind the caliper and pads does not cool off as fast as the rest of the exposed rotor.  This difference in cooling leads to the rotor warping.  I don't know how you could have avoided this except by taking off a hot caliper.  Not something I'd do.)<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Kwez||08-11-2001||08:31 PM||||raydar- <BR>we already tried the 'clean the mating surfaces' thing. <P>Lugs tension: the wheel was taken off / put back on a few times, by two different people, also, the prob was there after I got the car back from the tire shop. (assuming that they know how to properly put a wheel back on ! it's their specialty no ? ;-)<BR>That still could be it though.<P>primaris- the rotor that got hot was in the front & it is OK, no probs there.<P>The only way the rear rotors got warped is if the guys at the tire shop warped them with their post-installation tests. (but they LOOK ok, the one that got hot in the front was blueish)<P>Since the front brakes do more braking wouldn't they have warped before the rear anyways ? (or would be warped too)<P>Also, I was thinking: the rotor that's currently on the 'bad side' was Ok on the other side !!?? if it was warped I would've felt it on the other before ?<P>I love gremlins ;-)<P>I guess I'll have to try swaping rotors again, but front / rear this time.<BR>(don't know when that's gonna be possible though)<P>I appreciate all the help guys !<BR>||||reg||1
