Z||000011||Raydar||08-12-2001||10:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by starfighter007@msn.com:<BR><B>When you installed the new rotors did you have them machined after you took them out of the box before you installed them on the car??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Why do that? If they're new, they shouldn't need machining. I installed mine straight out of the box. I did take a rag soaked with lacquer thinner and wipe down the surfaces. They were coated with oil to prevent rust.<BR>BTW... new 88 Fiero Store rotors were $49/each. NAPA wanted $94.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||08-12-2001||10:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kwez:<BR><B>Lugs tension: the wheel was taken off / put back on a few times, by two different people, also, the prob was there after I got the car back from the tire shop. (assuming that they know how to properly put a wheel back on ! it's their specialty no ? ;-)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldn't count on a tire shop always knowing what they're doing. <BR>Did they use an impact wrench when they remounted your wheels?<BR>According to everything I've ever heard, aluminum wheels are supposed to be hand torqued. (Not that anybody ever does it.)<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Kwez||08-13-2001||02:37 PM||||raydar- i should of thought of 'doin' the wipe' ;-)<P>raydar- i honestly don't know about the way the shop remounted the wheels.<P>I'm actualy thinking of just dropping by and see if they have anything interesting to say about the prob. (hehe, maybe they'll offer to help since they gave me the car back in bad shape after putting on the rotrs & pads ? ... well, one can always dream can't they ? ;-)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||08-13-2001||03:47 PM||||If you don't feel like messing with the tire store right away, try this...<P>Jack one wheel off the ground. Loosen all the lugs on that wheel.<BR>Go back and tighten them, gradually, in a "star" pattern (meaning tighten every alternate lug, in a circle.) Tighten them all "snug" then go back around the circle and gradually tighten them all the way, still in the star pattern. The object of all this is to have them all tightened at the same rate, instead of having one "cranked down" while the others are still loose. <BR>Repeat this for the other wheels. <BR>It's a lot easier to do if you have a 4-way lug wrench. Don't try to kill them. Just get them tight. A 4-way can get them as tight as an impact wrench.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> The shop manuals recommend a torque wrench.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||87GTZ34||08-13-2001||05:00 PM||||Kwez,  what shape are your bearings in.  You mentioned that the problem gets worse as the car/brakes warm-up.  If the bearings are failing then the rotor will have movement within the caliper.  My suggestion would be to find a helper, get the car warmed up, jack one corner (use jackstands!!!) pull a wheel and watch the caliper 'grab' the rotor.  Look mainly to see that the pressure is even and nothing is twisting.  This would only reveal an obvious failure as there is no rotational force on the caliper (axel not turning) but you will be able to evaluate the caliper's clamping action. GL||||reg||1
Z||000016||starfighter007@msn.com||08-13-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kwez:<BR><B>machined ??<BR>no, why ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> Well what if the box was dropped between the manufacture of the rotor and the parts house. I ve learned you ALWAYS MACHINE BRAND NEW ROTORS.Where did I learn this you ask? At the ammco brake and front end school in walkegen Ill.Not sure if I spelled the town name right in case anyone cares to verify this. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||starfighter007@msn.com||08-13-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kwez:<BR><B>machined ??<BR>no, why ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> Well what if the box was dropped between the manufacture of the rotor and the parts house. I ve learned you ALWAYS MACHINE BRAND NEW ROTORS.Where did I learn this you ask? At the ammco brake and front end school in walkegen Ill.Not sure if I spelled the town name right in case anyone cares to verify this. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Kwez||08-13-2001||08:16 PM||||raydar- that's how I usually do it (well, almost, maybe I tighten the lugs a bit more than you suggest one the 1st round, but I'm pretty weak anyways, so same result ;-)<P>87GTZ34- I have no idea about the bearings' condition. Are you taking about the bearing that are <B>inside</B> the rotor ? because on the 88 there are none.<P>everybody-<BR>ok, i went back to the tire shop, explained the situation, he said "we're not busy, want us to have a look?" I said "OK".<BR>after a test drive, they put the car on the lift, removed the rear wheels, got a guy in the car to 'drive' & brake.<BR>We could see the caliper moving.<BR>He said: rotors, want to have 'em turned ?<BR>Me: sure, might as well.<P>After re-installing everything, test drive it & it did it once .. just once though.<BR>I drove the car for almost 2hrs after that and it seems ok.<P>Since a similar thing happened last time, I'll believe everything to be OK when I drive a few times & it doesn't come back.<P>hehe:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>machined ??<BR>no, why ?<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks again everybody, I'll keep you posted.<BR>I can drive my car now !!! yeee haaww !||||reg||1
Z||000019||Songman||08-13-2001||11:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B> <BR>Why do that? If they're new, they shouldn't need machining. I installed mine straight out of the box. I did take a rag soaked with lacquer thinner and wipe down the surfaces. They were coated with oil to prevent rust.<BR>BTW... new 88 Fiero Store rotors were $49/each. NAPA wanted $94.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And when my 88 was doing this (and it was definitely a warped rotor!) I went to Advance Auto parts and bought 94 Cavalier front rotors for <B>$13</B> each and replaced both rear rotors. No more problems. And I didn't machine them or wipe them down! hehe Straight from the box to the car..<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||14
Z||000020||Kwez||08-14-2001||06:55 PM||||ohhhh freak !<P>yep, you guessed right, still having probs.<BR>this time it's the front left !!<BR>it started doin' it today (drove the car for 2hrs yesterday & no probs !).<P>went back to the shop (they had offered to swap the rotors front-rear for free if I still had probs ;-)<P>the 'new' caliper (well, rebuilt caliper) is the culprit. the sleeves are too loose, you can wiggle 'em inside the caliper. it's actually the holes for the sleeves in the caliper that are bad.<BR>So, the guy that sold us the parts is supposed to fix it ...<BR>(he says there's no more calipers available !)<BR>if I'm lucky I should have the car back at the end of the day tomorrow. (?)||||reg||8
Z||000021||87GTBro||08-14-2001||09:50 PM||||NAPA sucks... I will never get parts from them again if I can help it.  I bought a new rear caliper from them for my 86 GT. The unloaded caliper cost $70.  The core charge was $107!!!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Kwez||08-15-2001||02:53 PM||||ok, back on track again.<P>the shop took care of getting the caliper to the 'rebuilder' where they bought it.<BR>they didn't charge me anything ...<P>I also learned that they had <B>given</B> me a set of new sleeves ($70CAD !) for the right front caliper 'cause it came w. half a caliper & no sleeves !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>everything looks fine for now ...<BR>I'll see in time.<BR>but I feel more confident this time.<P>Thanks again everybody.<BR>Keep smiling||||reg||6
